1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,147
Can a team of AI agents review a structural submittal better than one model working alone?

2
00:00:06,597 --> 00:00:09,242
I tried it on one of my evaluation tasks.

3
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:15,917
A pedestrian skybridge spans between a parking garage and an office building in Salt Lake City.

4
00:00:16,367 --> 00:00:21,295
At the garage end, it rests on a sliding bearing with 5 inches of travel each way.

5
00:00:21,745 --> 00:00:26,950
The fabricator's engineer says that is enough, and a junior engineer's draft review agrees.

6
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,389
Before signing, the engineer of record asks the model to check both.

7
00:00:32,389 --> 00:00:38,832
In team mode, the same model, Claude Sonnet 5.5, works as a review team in one shared channel.

8
00:00:39,282 --> 00:00:50,290
First, a code-basis agent pins down the rules: the 2024 IBC as adopted by Utah, ASCE 7-22 and ACI 318-19.

9
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:58,906
A lead splits the review into three parts: the seismic movement at the bearing, the bearing travel, and the corbel with the draft review.

10
00:00:59,906 --> 00:01:03,725
Three agents work in parallel, each with its own Python sandbox.

11
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:05,519
They catch real errors.

12
00:01:05,969 --> 00:01:13,862
The fabricator took the garage movement at the center of mass, not at the bearing, and used the office building's movement without amplifying it.

13
00:01:14,862 --> 00:01:17,422
Then they ask each other questions in the channel.

14
00:01:17,872 --> 00:01:26,875
They raise the critical doubt: if the current code calls for the MCE displacement, the pad runs off its stainless sheet.

15
00:01:27,325 --> 00:01:34,791
But the code-basis agent, recalling the 2016 edition from memory, says there is no such factor, and the team drops it.

16
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:44,730
The checker reads the whole channel and signs: revise and resubmit, but the bearing itself is adequate, with 3.66 inches of movement.

17
00:01:45,730 --> 00:01:47,010
That is the wrong call.

18
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:55,865
ASCE 7-22 requires the MCE displacement for a member that spans between two structures.

19
00:01:56,315 --> 00:02:00,859
That is about 6 inches with thermal movement, more than the 5 inches of travel.

20
00:02:01,309 --> 00:02:04,104
The team failed the task, just like Sonnet alone.

21
00:02:04,554 --> 00:02:07,818
With the code text as a look-up tool, Sonnet alone passed.

22
00:02:08,818 --> 00:02:12,167
More agents of the same model share the same blind spot.

23
00:02:12,617 --> 00:02:14,644
The governing text is what fixes it.

24
00:02:15,094 --> 00:02:19,723
The task, the full conversation and the harness are open source on GitHub.
